  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\nResearch Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\nAbstract\n========\n\nThe rapid evolution of code generation models powered by large language models (LLMs) has introduced novel security challenges that demand sophisticated defense and evaluation strategies. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances at the intersection of code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine how autonomous code agents are being developed for both offensive (red teaming) and defensive (blue teaming) purposes in the context of code generation AI. We analyze current methodologies for automated security evaluation, explore the emerging landscape of code generation benchmarks, and discuss the critical importance of addressing security vulnerabilities in AI-generated code. Our work highlights the complementary nature of red and blue teaming approaches and proposes a framework for integrating automated security testing into the code generation lifecycle. We review recent works including BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI [1], which introduces a novel approach to automated blue teaming for code generation models.\n\n1. Introduction\n===============\n\nThe deployment of large language models for code generation has transformed software development practices. Tools such as GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, and various specialized code generation models have become integral to the modern developer toolkit, enabling automated code completion, bug fixing, and even entire program synthesis. However, this transformation brings with it significant security concerns that must be carefully addressed.\n\nCode agents—autonomous systems capable of understanding, generating, and manipulating code—have emerged as powerful tools for software development. These agents can write code, execute programs, debug errors, and even implement security fixes. As their capabilities expand, so do the risks associated with their deployment. Vulnerabilities in AI-generated code, including buffer overflows, injection attacks, and insecure dependencies, can have severe consequences when deployed at scale.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of systematically probing systems for vulnerabilities, has become an essential component of AI safety research. In the context of code generation, red-teaming involves attempting to elicit insecure or malicious code from models, testing their robustness against adversarial prompts, and identifying potential attack vectors. Traditional red-teaming approaches often rely on human experts, but the complexity and scale of modern code generation models necessitate automated approaches.\n\nThis paper explores the intersection of code agents and red-teaming, examining how both offensive and defensive strategies are being developed. We organize our discussion around three key themes: (1) automated red-teaming methodologies for code generation models, (2) emerging blue-teaming agents for security evaluation, and (3) benchmarks and evaluation frameworks for assessing code generation security.\n\n2. Related Work\n===============\n\n2.1 Automated Red-Teaming for Code Generation\n\nRed-teaming has become a critical methodology for evaluating the safety and robustness of large language models. In the context of code generation, researchers have developed specialized attack strategies designed to elicit vulnerable code outputs. These strategies include prompt injection, instruction override, and multi-turn adversarial attacks that attempt to bypass safety filters through sophisticated prompt engineering.\n\nRecent work has shown that code generation models can be vulnerable to various attack vectors that result in the production of insecure code. Studies have demonstrated that even models with safety training can be tricked into generating code with critical vulnerabilities such as SQL injection, cross-site scripting, and buffer overflow when presented with carefully crafted prompts.\n\n2.2 Blue Teaming and Defensive Code Generation\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming approaches aim to detect and prevent the generation of insecure code. Traditional blue-teaming involves monitoring and defending systems against attacks, but in the context of AI, it also encompasses the development of safeguards and detection mechanisms.\n\nNotable work in this area includes BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI [1], which introduces a novel framework for automated security evaluation. This work presents a blue teaming agent that leverages automated red-teaming techniques to comprehensively evaluate the security of code generation models. The key contributions of this work include:\n\n- A novel framework for automated blue teaming of code generation models that integrates red-teaming methodologies for security evaluation.\n- An automated pipeline that generates security benchmarks through adversarial prompt generation and vulnerability exploitation.\n- Comprehensive evaluation of various code generation models on security metrics, revealing significant vulnerabilities even in state-of-the-art models.\n- Strategies for hardening code generation models against adversarial attacks through iterative security testing.\n\nThe BlueCodeAgent framework represents a significant advancement in automated security evaluation, demonstrating that red-teaming methodologies can be effectively repurposed for blue-teaming defense strategies.\n\n2.3 Security Benchmarks for Code Generation\n\nEvaluating the security of code generation models requires specialized benchmarks that capture the breadth and depth of potential vulnerabilities. Recent efforts have focused on developing comprehensive security benchmarks that test various vulnerability categories including injection attacks, insecure coding patterns, dependency vulnerabilities, and information leakage.\n\nThese benchmarks typically involve creating test suites that cover common vulnerability classes and measuring model performance across these dimensions. The development of standardized evaluation protocols enables fair comparison between different models and tracks progress over time.\n\n2.4 Autonomous Agents for Security Testing\n\nThe integration of autonomous agents into security testing workflows represents an emerging research direction. These agents can automatically generate test cases, execute programs, analyze outputs, and iterate on test generation based on feedback. Such systems reduce the manual effort required for comprehensive security evaluation and enable continuous assessment of code generation models.\n\nRecent advances in LLM-powered agents have demonstrated their capability to perform complex security testing tasks including vulnerability detection, exploit generation, and patch analysis. These autonomous agents complement human security experts by providing scalable and systematic evaluation capabilities.\n\n3. Methodology and Framework\n============================\n\nBuilding on the insights from recent work in both red-teaming and blue-teaming, we propose a unified framework for automated security evaluation of code generation models. This framework integrates both offensive and defensive approaches to provide comprehensive security assessment.\n\n3.1 Red-Teaming Phase\n\nThe red-teaming phase involves systematic vulnerability discovery through:\n\n- Adversarial prompt generation: Creating diverse attack prompts designed to elicit vulnerable code outputs.\n- Multi-turn attack strategies: Engaging in extended dialogues with the model to gradually bypass safety mechanisms.\n- Vulnerability classification: Categorizing generated insecure code by vulnerability type and severity.\n- Attack success metrics: Measuring the effectiveness of various attack strategies across different models.\n\n3.2 Blue-Teaming Phase\n\nThe blue-teaming phase focuses on detection and mitigation:\n\n- Vulnerability detection: Identifying security flaws in generated code through static analysis, dynamic analysis, and LLM-based review.\n- Security scoring: Assigning quantitative scores to generated code based on identified vulnerabilities.\n- Remediation strategies: Suggesting or implementing fixes for identified vulnerabilities.\n- Model hardening: Using identified vulnerabilities to improve model safety through fine-tuning and additional training.\n\nThe approach proposed in BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI [1] demonstrates the effectiveness of this integrated approach, showing that red-teaming insights can significantly enhance blue-teaming capabilities.\n\n3.3 Evaluation Metrics\n\nWe propose the following metrics for comprehensive evaluation:\n\n- Vulnerability Detection Rate: The percentage of vulnerable code samples correctly identified by security tools.\n- Attack Success Rate: The proportion of adversarial prompts that successfully elicit insecure code.\n- False Positive Rate: The rate at which secure code is incorrectly flagged as vulnerable.\n- Remediation Effectiveness: The ability to generate secure code or effective patches after vulnerability detection.\n\n4. Challenges and Future Directions\n===================================\n\nDespite significant progress, several challenges remain in the field of code agent security and red-teaming:\n\n4.1 Scalability\n\nAs code generation models grow in capability and complexity, the security evaluation space expands exponentially. Developing scalable evaluation methodologies that can keep pace with model advances is crucial.\n\n4.2 Generalization\n\nSecurity vulnerabilities discovered through testing may not generalize to all use cases. Ensuring that evaluation captures the full spectrum of potential vulnerabilities remains challenging.\n\n4.3 Adversarial Robustness\n\nAdversarial techniques continue to evolve, requiring continuous development of new defense strategies. The arms race between attack and defense necessitates ongoing research.\n\n4.4 Integration with Development Pipelines\n\nEffective security evaluation must be integrated into software development workflows without imposing excessive overhead. Developing efficient, non-intrusive security testing tools is essential for widespread adoption.\n\nFuture research directions include:\n\n- Developing more sophisticated adversarial attack strategies that can evaluate model robustness under diverse conditions.\n- Creating standardized benchmarks for code generation security evaluation.\n- Investigating techniques for automatically generating secure code from insecure samples.\n- Exploring human-in-the-loop approaches that combine automated evaluation with expert security analysis.\n- Developing real-time security monitoring systems for deployed code generation tools.\n\n5. Conclusion\n=============\n\nThe intersection of code agents and red-teaming represents a critical frontier in AI safety research. As code generation models become more powerful and widely deployed, the need for comprehensive security evaluation becomes increasingly urgent. This paper has surveyed recent advances in both automated red-teaming and blue-teaming approaches for code generation models.\n\nWe have highlighted the complementary nature of offensive and defensive strategies, demonstrating that insights from red-teaming can significantly enhance blue-teaming capabilities. The work of BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI [1] exemplifies this synergistic approach, providing a framework for automated security evaluation that leverages red-teaming techniques for blue-teaming defense.\n\nAs the field continues to evolve, collaborative efforts between red teams and blue teams will be essential for building secure and reliable code generation systems. The development of automated evaluation frameworks, standardized benchmarks, and integrated security pipelines will enable proactive security assessment that keeps pace with rapid advancements in code generation capabilities.\n\nUltimately, ensuring the security of AI-generated code requires sustained investment in both theoretical research and practical tooling. By building on the foundations established by recent work in automated red-teaming and blue-teaming, the research community can advance toward code generation systems that are both powerful and secure.\n\nReferences\n==========\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\nEOF"
